<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kharg Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kharg Island is a small island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> built a fort and trading post there in the 1750s, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-muhanna">Mir Muhanna</a>, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-rig">Bandar Rig</a>, seized in the 1760s. Since the 1960s it has been Iran's main crude-oil export terminal, and it was repeatedly bombed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kharg-island.jpg" alt="Kharg Island" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS005-E-11900_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kharg Island is a coral outcrop in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, about 25 to 28 kilometres off the Iranian coast and about 55 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a>, to whose province it belongs. It is around 8 kilometres long and 4 to 5 kilometres wide. The surrounding waters are deep, and it is one of the few islands in the Gulf with its own supply of fresh water. The island's name has also been recorded as Khark, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">Kharaj</a> and Kharej.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Hudud al-'Alam mentions Kharg around 982 CE as a source of pearls, and it served as a stopover for ships sailing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>. The geographer Yaqut visited in 1218. The Portuguese claimed it in 1507 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tristao-da-cunha">Tristão da Cunha</a>. In 1753 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> agent Baron Tiddo Frederik van Kniphausen paid 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> to Mir Nasáir, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-rig">Bandar Rig</a>, for perpetual ownership, and made the island a free port. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> built a trading post and Fort Mosselstein. A church was built in 1757, and a Carmelite priest served what was then the largest Catholic community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><p>In January 1766, after relations with local peoples worsened, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-muhanna">Mir Muhanna</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-rig">Bandar Rig</a>, seized the fortress and forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> out for good. The British occupied the island briefly in 1837 and again from December 1856, during the Anglo-Persian War. Reza Shah Pahlavi used Kharg as a place of exile for political opponents. Work on oil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> began in 1956, and a terminal fed by a pipeline from the Gachsaran field was inaugurated on 8 November 1960. By around 1975 it was the world's largest offshore crude-oil terminal. The Iraqi air force bombed it heavily between 1982 and 1986.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Kharg holds a number of archaeological sites. In 2007 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a>, dated to between 550 and 330 BCE, was found carved on a coral rock. Its authenticity and meaning have been disputed, and it was damaged in 2008. A ruined Christian complex, the Kharg Monastery, measures about 96 by 85 metres and has a chapel and 19 monks' cells. Daniel T. Potts dates it from ceramic evidence to the 8th and 9th centuries, while Marie-Joseph Stève suggested a founding as early as the 7th century. Rock-cut tombs, megalithic tombs and Zoroastrian and Christian burials have also been studied.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kharg Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1763 he granted the British East India Company the right to establish a factory at Bushire (Bushehr) on the Persian Gulf coast, while the Dutch East India Company maintained a post on <b>Kharg Island</b> until it was lost to the Gulf chieftain Mir Muhanna in 1766.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of Nader Shah in 1747 central authority collapsed, and Mir Nasir governed the port as a local lord with links to the Dutch East India Company on nearby <b>Kharg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-bandar-rig">Lordship of Bandar Rig</a></li></ul></section>
